WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.400
I want you to picture your own body right now, but I don't want you to think of it as

00:05.400 --> 00:10.520
this solid, static object, you know, like the way we usually look at ourselves in the

00:10.520 --> 00:10.720
mirror.

00:11.040 --> 00:11.100
Right.

00:11.640 --> 00:19.520
Instead, I want you to imagine it as a column of water, just a literal column of water temporarily

00:19.520 --> 00:25.820
held in a specific precarious shape by a remarkably tiny amount of mineral and protein.

00:26.100 --> 00:27.440
That is a weird image, I know.

00:27.500 --> 00:28.940
It sounds super strange.

00:29.900 --> 00:34.500
But that is the fundamental physical reality of what you are.

00:34.900 --> 00:39.560
And it is the starting point for a deep dive today that is, I mean, it's going to completely

00:39.560 --> 00:41.860
change how you view your own biology.

00:42.620 --> 00:45.660
It really represents a profound shift in perspective.

00:45.660 --> 00:50.600
Like when you stop looking at the body as a machine made of, you know, solid interchangeable

00:50.600 --> 00:57.120
parts and you start seeing it as a boundary managed volume of fluid, the boundaries themselves

00:57.120 --> 00:59.400
actually become the most important part of the story.

00:59.680 --> 00:59.920
Wow.

01:00.740 --> 01:00.980
Yeah.

01:01.080 --> 01:05.700
Because the core question here isn't just what sort of futuristic technology we can build

01:05.700 --> 01:07.320
to replace our failing organs.

01:07.680 --> 01:12.140
The fundamental question is why a biological boundary matters in the first place.

01:12.260 --> 01:12.600
Right.

01:12.600 --> 01:18.940
And, well, what we stand to lose mathematically and physiologically when we start outsourcing

01:18.940 --> 01:20.280
our survival to machines.

01:21.160 --> 01:22.480
Okay, let's unpack this.

01:22.480 --> 01:28.240
We are diving into a deeply fascinating, heavily speculative graduate textbook from November

01:28.240 --> 01:29.260
2026.

01:29.900 --> 01:34.620
It was written by an independent researcher who goes by the name Flyxion, and it's titled

01:34.620 --> 01:37.800
Artificial Organs and Transhuman Trajectories.

01:38.040 --> 01:38.600
That's the one.

01:38.600 --> 01:40.600
The scope of this text is massive.

01:40.920 --> 01:46.240
Like, it explores systems physiology and engineered trajectories for the human body over a hundred

01:46.240 --> 01:48.460
to a thousand year horizon.

01:48.780 --> 01:52.100
Which is an incredible time span to even try to model.

01:52.500 --> 01:52.960
Seriously.

01:53.540 --> 01:57.460
And the mission of this deep dive is to figure out exactly how much of the biological machinery

01:57.460 --> 02:02.540
that sustains our lives could realistically be relocated outside our bodies.

02:03.040 --> 02:03.540
Exactly.

02:04.020 --> 02:07.140
Perhaps into our clothes or, you know, into portable habitats.

02:07.140 --> 02:14.080
We are going to look at the exact physics, biology, and engineering of what makes you, you.

02:14.960 --> 02:20.520
And what sets this text apart from standard speculative futurism is the rigorous grounding.

02:20.780 --> 02:25.220
I mean, the author grounds every single speculation in hard thermodynamic limits.

02:25.420 --> 02:26.280
No magic wands here.

02:26.420 --> 02:26.620
Right.

02:26.800 --> 02:29.180
It's all fluid dynamics and control theory.

02:29.420 --> 02:31.760
It poses a highly technical question.

02:31.760 --> 02:39.580
If you actually want to become a transhuman entity or, you know, even just heavily rely on wearable artificial organs

02:39.580 --> 02:42.460
to extend your life, how does the math actually work?

02:43.120 --> 02:43.200
Right.

02:43.280 --> 02:46.080
Because we aren't talking about magic sci-fi nanobots.

02:46.480 --> 02:52.360
We are talking about the unavoidable physics of heat, mass transfer, and biological rejection.

02:52.800 --> 02:53.300
Precisely.

02:53.540 --> 02:55.240
You can't just wave away the physics.

02:55.240 --> 03:00.100
Which brings us to this wet animal concept from the first chapter.

03:00.780 --> 03:02.740
I mentioned the column of water earlier.

03:03.060 --> 03:07.260
The book notes that roughly 60% of an adult male body is water.

03:07.900 --> 03:10.680
Which is a staggering amount when you really think about it.

03:10.960 --> 03:11.340
It is.

03:11.720 --> 03:14.560
That's about 42 liters, or roughly 11 gallons.

03:14.560 --> 03:21.520
If you are listening to this right now and weigh around 70 kilograms, you're carrying around 11 gallons of water.

03:22.020 --> 03:22.200
Yeah.

03:22.200 --> 03:24.740
But where is all that fluid actually sitting?

03:25.200 --> 03:28.960
Because when I move, I don't exactly hear 11 gallons sloshing around in my chest.

03:29.400 --> 03:29.500
Right.

03:29.600 --> 03:33.780
And the reason you don't hear sloshing is due to intense compartmentalization.

03:34.060 --> 03:34.300
Okay.

03:34.460 --> 03:38.700
About two-thirds of that 42 liters is locked entirely inside your cells.

03:38.840 --> 03:40.280
That's the intracellular fluid.

03:40.620 --> 03:40.840
Okay.

03:40.900 --> 03:43.440
So mostly it's just inside the individual cells.

03:43.720 --> 03:44.180
Exactly.

03:44.440 --> 03:44.640
Okay.

03:44.860 --> 03:51.260
The rest is extracellular, which is divided between your circulating blood plasma and something called the interstitial fluid.

03:51.720 --> 03:52.680
Interstitial fluid.

03:53.100 --> 03:53.260
Yeah.

03:53.380 --> 03:56.340
And this interstitial fluid is absolutely crucial to understand.

03:56.720 --> 04:03.540
Imagine it as a very slow-moving, highly regulated ocean that bathes almost every single cell in your body.

04:03.980 --> 04:06.580
Like a microscopic ocean everywhere inside you.

04:06.740 --> 04:07.220
Exactly.

04:07.460 --> 04:18.760
Every single molecule of oxygen you breathe, every nutrient from your food, every signaling hormone, and every piece of metabolic waste, it all has to physically pass through that interstitial fluid.

04:18.760 --> 04:21.860
It has to go from your capillaries to your cells and back again.

04:22.140 --> 04:27.100
See, I always pictured blood vessels just plugging directly into cells like, I don't know, like a power cord.

04:27.560 --> 04:28.920
But you're saying there's a moat.

04:29.500 --> 04:32.140
The interstitial fluid is this ultimate transit zone.

04:32.500 --> 04:34.780
That moat is the perfect way to look at it.

04:34.780 --> 04:43.020
And any implanted medical device you put into the body that isn't sitting directly inside a blood vessel is going to be sitting in that interstitial fluid.

04:43.380 --> 04:43.600
Oh, wow.

04:43.760 --> 04:45.200
It has to interact with that ocean.

04:45.380 --> 04:46.180
You can't avoid it.

04:46.180 --> 04:52.300
Which leads to what I think is the most mind-bending idea in the first chapter, the illusion of inside.

04:52.480 --> 04:53.520
Oh, this is a great concept.

04:53.780 --> 04:53.940
Yeah.

04:54.360 --> 05:01.700
The text argues that the human body has far less true interior than those anatomical diagrams from middle school biology led us to believe.

05:01.780 --> 05:06.880
Like the gut, the airways, the bladder, they are technically continuous with the outside world.

05:06.880 --> 05:14.880
Yeah, if you strip away the arms and legs and just look at the geometry of a human being, we are essentially a very complex torus.

05:15.080 --> 05:15.700
A torus.

05:15.800 --> 05:16.380
Like a donut.

05:16.620 --> 05:17.060
Exactly.

05:17.580 --> 05:18.000
A donut.

05:18.420 --> 05:20.960
The hole in the middle of that donut is your digestive tract.

05:21.540 --> 05:26.060
So the food you swallow is not technically inside your body yet.

05:26.320 --> 05:27.660
That's so weird to think about.

05:27.960 --> 05:30.520
It's still occupying the hole of the donut.

05:30.520 --> 05:38.040
It only enters your true biological interior when it crosses the cellular boundary, the epithelium of your gut.

05:38.740 --> 05:40.360
Think about that morning cup of coffee you had today.

05:40.900 --> 05:45.860
As it's sitting in your stomach, just pooling there, it is technically still outside of you.

05:46.060 --> 05:46.240
Yep.

05:46.480 --> 05:51.400
It's not truly inside your body until it actively crosses a selective mucosal barrier.

05:52.060 --> 05:53.840
And the text spends a lot of time on this barrier.

05:54.380 --> 05:54.960
It does.

05:54.960 --> 06:03.720
It describes epithelia and mucus not just as passive walls, keeping the bad stuff out, but as highly active, fiercely selective boundaries.

06:04.320 --> 06:09.900
Right, because we tend to think of mucus as just inert slime, you know, like something you cough up in your sec.

06:10.300 --> 06:10.780
Exactly.

06:11.060 --> 06:15.940
But the text describes native mucus as a heavily glycosylated hydrogel.

06:16.560 --> 06:21.200
Okay, let's break that down because heavily glycosylated hydrogel is definitely a mouthful.

06:21.420 --> 06:21.800
It is.

06:21.800 --> 06:27.040
So a hydrogel is a network of polymer chains that are incredibly absorbent.

06:27.340 --> 06:31.600
They hold massive amounts of water while maintaining a solid-like structure.

06:31.980 --> 06:32.760
Okay, that makes sense.

06:33.180 --> 06:37.040
And the glycosylated part means these proteins are heavily coated in sugars.

06:37.340 --> 06:43.240
This sugar coating creates a sticky, incredibly complex, and highly engineered chemical mesh.

06:43.400 --> 06:45.020
So it's not just a physical wall.

06:45.020 --> 06:45.780
Not at all.

06:46.100 --> 06:47.700
It traps viral particles.

06:48.160 --> 06:49.840
It lubricates the movement of food.

06:50.100 --> 06:53.780
And it maintains strict layered gradients of oxygen and pH.

06:53.940 --> 06:54.120
Wow.

06:54.360 --> 07:02.460
The acidity at the top of the mucus layer can be completely different from the acidity just a fraction of a millimeter below it, right where it touches the tissue.

07:02.720 --> 07:04.220
And it isn't static, right?

07:04.340 --> 07:04.560
No.

07:04.680 --> 07:05.840
It renews itself constantly.

07:05.840 --> 07:12.500
Like, the text points out that in your airways, tiny hair-like structures called cilia sweep the mucus up and out.

07:12.760 --> 07:12.980
Yep.

07:13.200 --> 07:15.840
Renewing the entire layer every few tens of minutes.

07:15.920 --> 07:17.040
That's incredibly fast.

07:17.380 --> 07:17.660
Mm-hmm.

07:17.860 --> 07:20.200
And in the intestines, it turns over every few hours.

07:20.540 --> 07:24.660
And this rapid turnover is an absolute nightmare for artificial organs.

07:24.900 --> 07:25.440
Oh, I bet.

07:25.440 --> 07:35.960
Yeah, if you try to introduce an artificial interface, say, like a permanent port that crosses a mucosal surface to deliver nutrients or remove waste, you aren't just punching a hole in drywall.

07:36.140 --> 07:36.460
Right.

07:37.000 --> 07:42.820
You are constantly perturbing a living, rapidly renewing, fiercely defensive ecosystem.

07:43.500 --> 07:48.920
The body will constantly try to push the device out, seal it off, or just straight up degrade it with enzymes.

07:49.260 --> 07:54.660
This fluid reality completely changes how we categorize our organs, I think.

07:54.660 --> 08:00.680
The author suggests we need to stop looking at organs by their physical names, like we should stop saying kidney or liver.

08:01.240 --> 08:03.040
Which is hard to do because that's how we're all taught.

08:03.260 --> 08:03.400
Yeah.

08:03.820 --> 08:07.720
But instead, we need to look at anatomy as an allocation of work.

08:08.000 --> 08:09.720
We should be looking at allocation matrices.

08:10.360 --> 08:10.740
Exactly.

08:11.120 --> 08:12.800
The body is just a series of operations.

08:13.320 --> 08:17.700
Sensing, pumping, sorting, storing, contracting, signaling, repairing.

08:18.040 --> 08:23.580
And approaching biology through an allocation matrix is an incredibly powerful engineering framework.

08:23.700 --> 08:24.000
How so?

08:24.000 --> 08:29.420
Well, instead of saying, we need to build a mechanical kidney, you look at the matrix.

08:29.920 --> 08:33.300
The kidney's job is a sorting operation for urea and water.

08:33.620 --> 08:36.980
It's an endocrine signaling operation for hormones like renin.

08:37.300 --> 08:39.640
And a sensing operation for oxygen delivery.

08:40.000 --> 08:42.260
So it's doing multiple discrete tasks.

08:42.540 --> 08:42.980
Exactly.

08:42.980 --> 08:49.580
When you break anatomy into these discrete operations, you suddenly realize you don't need a single kidney-shaped box.

08:49.900 --> 08:50.000
Right.

08:50.200 --> 08:56.280
You can reallocate those operations to completely different parts of the body or, you know, even move some of them outside the body entirely.

08:56.280 --> 09:02.040
I was just blown away by the five specific kinds of engineering interventions the text breaks down based on this matrix.

09:02.700 --> 09:05.620
I always thought about organ replacement as just a one-to-one swap.

09:05.620 --> 09:08.280
But the author outlines five distinct pathways.

09:08.800 --> 09:09.580
Let's walk through them.

09:09.580 --> 09:09.940
Sure.

09:10.540 --> 09:11.960
The first pathway is replacement.

09:12.160 --> 09:13.200
This is what you were just mentioning.

09:13.760 --> 09:17.480
A substitute takes over the entire column of an organ's matrix.

09:18.320 --> 09:21.380
A cochlear implant is a perfect historical example of this.

09:21.540 --> 09:26.720
It entirely replaces the signal transduction operation of damaged hair cells in the ear.

09:26.720 --> 09:28.520
It takes over the job completely.

09:28.980 --> 09:29.020
Okay.

09:29.200 --> 09:37.240
So then there is supplementation, which is adding capacity to an existing structure that's struggling rather than replacing it outright.

09:37.780 --> 09:39.800
I guess the classic example here would be a pacemaker.

09:40.080 --> 09:43.060
A pacemaker is a prime example of supplementation.

09:43.420 --> 09:48.200
The heart's native electrical node is failing, so the pacemaker supplements the signaling operation.

09:48.380 --> 09:49.060
Oh, that makes sense.

09:49.060 --> 09:56.860
Another one would be a ventricular assist device, which is essentially a mechanical pump attached to a weakened heart to help it push blood.

09:57.180 --> 09:58.420
You keep the original organ.

09:58.660 --> 10:00.140
You just give it an engine boost.

10:00.820 --> 10:07.400
The third one is externalization, where you route the body's fluids outside to perform an operation.

10:08.300 --> 10:10.260
Hemodialysis is the obvious one here, right?

10:11.060 --> 10:17.100
We literally pump a person's blood out of their body, run it through an external sorting operation, and pump it back in.

10:17.100 --> 10:21.560
And dialysis highlights the sheer volume of fluid we are talking about.

10:22.120 --> 10:30.320
To clean the blood, you are moving massive quantities of liquid across a synthetic boundary, which requires immense amounts of fluid dynamics management.

10:30.480 --> 10:31.240
It's no small feat.

10:31.820 --> 10:34.800
Now, the fourth intervention completely broke my brain.

10:35.840 --> 10:36.320
Redistribution.

10:36.840 --> 10:37.160
Oh, yeah.

10:37.160 --> 10:48.980
This is where you take a function normally concentrated in one organ and spread it out over many tiny nodes across the body, like taking a kidney and spreading it out over a thousand tiny implants.

10:49.180 --> 10:50.460
It's wild to think about.

10:50.860 --> 10:53.740
How does that not immediately trigger a massive immune response?

10:54.140 --> 10:57.460
We'll get into the architecture of that later, but the concept is just wild.

10:57.720 --> 11:00.800
It totally challenges the very idea of localized anatomy.

11:00.800 --> 11:04.980
And finally, the fifth intervention is addition.

11:05.400 --> 11:10.020
This is creating an entirely new biological function that evolution never provided.

11:10.480 --> 11:12.500
We aren't replacing or supplementing.

11:12.600 --> 11:13.460
We are adding.

11:14.020 --> 11:23.200
Like adding a magnetic sensor to your nervous system so you can feel north, or, you know, a system to continuously recover water from your own sweat so you never have to drink.

11:23.480 --> 11:25.580
Those are prime examples of addition.

11:26.100 --> 11:28.360
But this brings up a major hurdle.

11:28.360 --> 11:29.040
Right.

11:29.180 --> 11:34.140
I mean, if the body is just an allocation of work, it sounds like re-orging a corporate company.

11:34.420 --> 11:34.600
Yeah.

11:34.880 --> 11:42.480
But you can't just fire the accounting department, let's call them the kidneys, and outsource the work to a cloud server in another country without massive lag.

11:43.300 --> 11:44.860
Doesn't physical location matter?

11:45.620 --> 11:51.320
I mean, if you put a chemical sensor in my toe to regulate the blood pressure in my brain, the delay would be catastrophic.

11:51.320 --> 11:53.480
Location matters immensely.

11:53.740 --> 11:59.280
And that is exactly where the physical engineering constraints slam into the biological reality.

11:59.960 --> 12:06.360
You cannot just swap operations around freely because of the staggering scale of the body as a chemical ecosystem.

12:06.740 --> 12:13.500
The author calculates the sheer volume of work the body does at rest, and the numbers are just mind-bending.

12:13.960 --> 12:14.660
Tell me about it.

12:14.660 --> 12:21.120
Let's examine ATP, adenosine triphosphate, which is the fundamental energy currency of our cells.

12:21.660 --> 12:26.580
The text mentions we turn over 80 kilograms of ATP daily.

12:27.080 --> 12:29.480
When I read that, I had to stop and reread the page.

12:30.080 --> 12:31.280
It's a huge number.

12:31.740 --> 12:34.800
80 kilograms is roughly 176 pounds.

12:34.940 --> 12:36.720
That is more than my entire body weight.

12:36.720 --> 12:41.100
How is that physically possible if I don't weigh 80 kilograms more at the end of the day?

12:41.200 --> 12:41.800
Where does it go?

12:42.640 --> 12:47.800
Well, it doesn't go anywhere in terms of mass because it is a closed cycle of hydrolysis and resynthesis.

12:48.420 --> 12:48.460
Okay.

12:49.040 --> 12:52.740
ATP is an adenosine molecule with three phosphates attached.

12:53.360 --> 13:01.460
When a cell needs energy, like to contract a muscle fiber or pump an ion across a membrane, it breaks off the third phosphate.

13:01.860 --> 13:04.060
Breaking that bond is what releases energy.

13:04.320 --> 13:04.620
Yes.

13:04.620 --> 13:06.580
That process is hydrolysis.

13:06.720 --> 13:18.140
And then the mitochondria, you know, the powerhouses of the cell, have to take that broken molecule and slap the phosphate back on, using energy from the food we eat to make it ready to use again.

13:18.640 --> 13:19.080
Precisely.

13:19.580 --> 13:25.300
You only actually store a few tens of grams of ATP in your entire body at any given moment.

13:25.440 --> 13:28.640
But you are constantly breaking those bonds and rebuilding them.

13:28.800 --> 13:29.040
Wow.

13:29.520 --> 13:33.460
It is not the total physical inventory of ATP that matters.

13:33.460 --> 13:36.000
It is the rate of the reaction events.

13:36.320 --> 13:36.580
The rate.

13:36.720 --> 13:43.200
Your body is executing roughly 10 to the 21st power reaction events every single second.

13:43.500 --> 13:44.800
One with 21 zeros.

13:45.200 --> 13:50.720
A billion trillion chemical reactions every single second just to keep me sitting here breathing.

13:50.920 --> 13:53.140
And it is not just the sheer number of reactions.

13:53.360 --> 13:55.200
It's the physical fallout from them.

13:55.360 --> 13:56.560
Which is heat, right?

13:57.080 --> 13:57.640
Exactly.

13:58.100 --> 14:02.140
The process of breaking and rebuilding those bonds is thermodynamically inefficient.

14:02.140 --> 14:03.640
It produces waste heat.

14:03.640 --> 14:04.200
Got it.

14:04.200 --> 14:14.800
That staggering event rate dictates our enormous, unrelenting demand for heat dissipation, for water to carry that heat, and for oxygen to fuel the rebuilding process.

14:14.800 --> 14:15.280
Oh.

14:15.280 --> 14:21.880
If you decide to outsource a biological function to a machine, you are inheriting the physics of that massive event rate.

14:21.880 --> 14:30.140
So if we mathematically cannot put a high-energy machine inside the body because of this event rate, we have to look at the accounting.

14:30.140 --> 14:31.860
Like, you can't cheat physics.

14:32.200 --> 14:34.660
Let's transition to the metabolic ledger.

14:35.000 --> 14:35.240
Right.

14:35.340 --> 14:36.200
The accounting side.

14:36.360 --> 14:41.980
Because if we are talking about outsourcing our survival, we need a strict accounting of what goes in and what goes out.

14:42.420 --> 14:49.200
The author sets up a reference adult, 70 kilograms resting in a temperate climate, to establish a baseline ledger.

14:49.200 --> 14:53.040
In engineering any closed system, you have to track the mass balance.

14:53.480 --> 14:53.780
Makes sense.

14:54.420 --> 14:57.380
The water ledger is usually what we focus on intuitively.

14:58.160 --> 15:09.520
The reference adult takes in about 2.6 liters of water a day through drinking and moisture and food, and loses about 2.6 liters through urine, sweat, the moisture in our breath, and feces.

15:10.040 --> 15:13.620
It seems like liquid is the main mass we exchange with the world.

15:13.840 --> 15:14.780
It does seem that way.

15:14.780 --> 15:18.240
But if you look at the gas ledger, it's a completely different story.

15:18.860 --> 15:26.040
This reference person consumes 0.5 kilograms of oxygen and produces 0.6 kilograms of carbon dioxide every day.

15:26.300 --> 15:27.840
The math there is just striking.

15:28.200 --> 15:33.380
I feel like I constantly need a glass of water, but I never think about the sheer weight of the air I'm breathing.

15:33.920 --> 15:37.060
We exchange more mass in gas than we do in liquid water.

15:37.480 --> 15:40.040
We are literally breathing out our own weight.

15:40.480 --> 15:43.600
Yeah, we exchange over a kilogram of gas mass a day.

15:43.600 --> 15:48.080
This ledger habit forces us to track every single chemical stream.

15:48.680 --> 16:01.960
If you are going to design a sealed spacesuit or a transhuman integument, you know, a permanent wearable bodysuit, you have to account for where every single gram of that carbon dioxide goes, and where every joule of heat goes.

16:02.340 --> 16:05.200
You cannot just ignore a stream because it is invisible.

16:05.500 --> 16:05.900
Exactly.

16:06.440 --> 16:07.280
You can't ignore anything.

16:07.280 --> 16:12.520
And this leads us directly to the holy grail of survival engineering, water recovery.

16:13.520 --> 16:14.320
Oh, yeah, the big one.

16:14.500 --> 16:22.540
If I want to wear my kidney on my belt or recover all my sweat so I never need to drink again, I need a way to separate pure water from the dissolved wastes.

16:23.040 --> 16:28.360
The text completely dismantles a very common science fiction trope here, the miniaturized centrifuge.

16:28.360 --> 16:32.020
It is a brilliant, necessary takedown of bad sci-fi physics.

16:32.320 --> 16:33.840
I love how they debunk this.

16:33.840 --> 16:42.640
In fiction, you constantly see these tiny, high-tech centrifuges on someone's wrists spinning rapidly to purify toxic water into drinking water.

16:43.200 --> 16:46.100
But the physics of centrifuges do not work that way.

16:46.300 --> 16:46.400
Right.

16:46.800 --> 16:49.700
Centrifuges sort particles based on density differences.

16:49.700 --> 16:57.960
They are fantastic at spinning out heavy suspended objects like red blood cells from plasma or bacteria or precipitated crystals.

16:58.740 --> 17:00.360
But they cannot desiccate fluids.

17:01.080 --> 17:03.960
They cannot separate dissolved solutes from the water itself.

17:04.200 --> 17:04.320
No.

17:04.960 --> 17:09.520
Dissolved molecules like urea or salt are fundamentally different from suspended particles.

17:09.900 --> 17:12.780
The author does the math on this, and it is staggering.

17:13.420 --> 17:17.860
Let's say you had an ultracentrifuge pulling 100,000 times the force of gravity.

17:17.860 --> 17:22.300
Which, by the way, is a massive, highly dangerous piece of industrial equipment.

17:23.060 --> 17:26.880
A rotor failure at 100,000 Gs is essentially a bomb going off.

17:27.320 --> 17:27.560
Right.

17:28.020 --> 17:29.740
But let's pretend we have a magic sci-fi one.

17:30.420 --> 17:38.260
Even at 100,000 Gs, it would only produce a 6% concentration difference for dissolved urea across a distance of one centimeter.

17:38.760 --> 17:39.580
It's basically nothing.

17:39.980 --> 17:45.520
You run this incredibly powerful machine, and the water is only 6% cleaner on one side than the other.

17:45.880 --> 17:47.180
Why is it so ineffective?

17:47.180 --> 17:51.220
Because of thermal motion, specifically Brownian motion.

17:51.700 --> 17:58.820
Dissolved molecules are so incredibly tiny that the ambient heat of the liquid causes them to constantly jitter and bounce around.

17:59.040 --> 18:00.560
So the heat is fighting the spin.

18:01.000 --> 18:01.420
Exactly.

18:01.700 --> 18:07.780
That random thermal bouncing force completely counteracts the centrifugal force trying to push them to one side.

18:08.140 --> 18:10.620
You are fighting the very heat of the fluid itself.

18:10.880 --> 18:14.240
So you literally cannot spin water pure.

18:14.820 --> 18:15.860
The physics won't allow it.

18:15.860 --> 18:20.580
To separate dissolved wastes, you have to use membranes, phase changes, or chemical affinity.

18:20.860 --> 18:21.100
Okay.

18:21.460 --> 18:27.540
You have to use reverse osmosis, where you physically force water through pores so small the urea gets stuck,

18:27.540 --> 18:32.280
or distillation, where you boil the water into vapor and recondense it.

18:32.720 --> 18:34.320
Which raises an obvious question.

18:34.480 --> 18:34.740
Okay.

18:34.740 --> 18:38.460
Why not just build a tiny distillation unit and implant it inside the body?

18:39.020 --> 18:40.240
Make it the size of a golf ball?

18:40.900 --> 18:45.840
Plug it into the bladder and just let it boil the urine and recondense the pure water back into the bloodstream?

18:45.840 --> 18:52.020
What's fascinating here is where the limits of miniaturization hit a thermodynamic brick wall.

18:52.020 --> 18:52.500
Okay.

18:52.900 --> 18:58.120
It is vital to understand that shrinking a component completely changes its relationship to heat.

18:58.780 --> 19:02.120
Let's look at the tissue heating limit the author calculates.

19:02.260 --> 19:03.240
Yes, let's look at that.

19:03.240 --> 19:09.020
Say you build a tiny 4 cubic centimeter internal water distillation unit.

19:09.420 --> 19:11.040
4 cubic centimeters is tiny.

19:11.400 --> 19:12.240
Like a large marble.

19:12.580 --> 19:13.000
Exactly.

19:13.720 --> 19:17.000
Now, even assuming incredibly efficient heat recovery mechanisms,

19:17.300 --> 19:21.740
to process 1.5 liters of water a day through phase change,

19:21.960 --> 19:28.360
that little marble would need to constantly dissipate about 1.7 watts of waste heat.

19:28.360 --> 19:31.080
Honestly, 1.7 watts does not sound like a lot.

19:31.520 --> 19:35.720
An old incandescent light bulb that you would put in a bedside lamp is 60 watts.

19:36.000 --> 19:41.860
It doesn't sound like much until you compress it into a tiny sphere and implant it deeply into biological tissue.

19:42.300 --> 19:47.120
The surface area of a 4 cubic centimeter sphere is incredibly small.

19:47.180 --> 19:47.380
Right.

19:47.700 --> 19:50.100
And heat can only escape an object through its surface.

19:50.480 --> 19:52.180
So the heat bottlenecks at the surface.

19:52.660 --> 19:53.220
Exactly.

19:53.220 --> 19:58.900
The author calculates that a sphere this size dissipating just 1 watt of power

19:58.900 --> 20:04.120
would push the temperature of the surrounding biological tissue up by 16 Kelvin.

20:04.580 --> 20:05.460
16 Kelvin.

20:05.820 --> 20:09.380
For those used to Celsius, a change in Kelvin is a 1 to 1 scale.

20:09.820 --> 20:12.500
So that is a 16 degrees Celsius increase.

20:12.760 --> 20:13.000
Yes.

20:13.180 --> 20:15.860
Human body temperature is about 37 degrees Celsius.

20:16.500 --> 20:21.780
Pushing that up by 16 degrees puts the surrounding tissue at 53 degrees Celsius.

20:21.780 --> 20:25.840
You would literally cook your own internal organs from the inside out.

20:26.040 --> 20:28.440
It would cause massive thermal necrosis.

20:28.940 --> 20:30.380
The tissue would die rapidly.

20:31.140 --> 20:35.600
Shrinking a device does not shrink the amount of heat it produces to do a specific job.

20:35.780 --> 20:40.340
It just concentrates that exact same amount of heat into a much smaller surface area.

20:40.900 --> 20:47.920
So shrinking a high energy medical device is like putting a high-end gaming PC into a tiny sealed shoebox without a cooling fan.

20:47.920 --> 20:55.740
The processing power is technically all there, but because the heat has nowhere to go, it will literally melt its own motherboard.

20:56.080 --> 20:57.140
That is a perfect analogy.

20:57.920 --> 21:00.720
You have concentrated the work, but ignored the dissipation.

21:01.000 --> 21:01.280
Right.

21:01.480 --> 21:08.120
And the thermodynamic work required to separate water from a 600 milliosmol urine solution is worth discussing here.

21:08.300 --> 21:09.980
Let's define that term for the listener.

21:10.300 --> 21:10.920
600 milliosmol.

21:10.920 --> 21:11.920
Sure.

21:12.340 --> 21:16.080
Osmolarity is just a measure of how crowded a fluid is with dissolved particles.

21:16.380 --> 21:21.720
A 600 milliosmol solution is roughly twice as concentrated as your normal blood plasma.

21:22.200 --> 21:23.960
It's relatively concentrated urine.

21:24.360 --> 21:24.580
Okay.

21:24.780 --> 21:25.100
Got it.

21:25.460 --> 21:33.200
Now, the absolute minimum thermodynamic energy required to pull 1.5 liters of pure water out of that solution is incredibly small.

21:33.400 --> 21:34.840
Only about 6 kilojoules.

21:34.840 --> 21:39.360
6 kilojoules is roughly the energy you get from eating a quarter of a peanut?

21:39.740 --> 21:40.120
Exactly.

21:40.900 --> 21:45.980
The energy required to physically break the chemical affinity between the water and the waste isn't the problem.

21:46.240 --> 21:47.040
It's the byproduct.

21:47.380 --> 21:47.580
Right.

21:47.760 --> 21:53.340
The real barrier to building an artificial kidney is the gross inefficiency of the microscopic pumps you'd need.

21:53.880 --> 21:56.480
The rapid fouling of the filtration membranes by proteins,

21:57.080 --> 22:00.460
the storage of the highly toxic concentrated brine you are left with,

22:00.460 --> 22:04.240
and most importantly, the waste heat generated by all those inefficient components.

22:04.240 --> 22:10.420
There is another miniaturization limit the text mentions related to our circulatory system, the Krogh cylinder.

22:10.780 --> 22:11.560
Oh, this is crucial.

22:12.220 --> 22:18.800
It's really as to why we can't just have one central oxygen node implanted in our chest that beams oxygen to all our cells.

22:19.040 --> 22:23.920
Right, because oxygen diffuses through biological tissue very, very slowly.

22:23.920 --> 22:29.320
The Krogh model calculates the absolute maximum distance a cell can be from a blood capillary

22:29.320 --> 22:34.000
and still receive enough oxygen via passive diffusion to survive.

22:34.240 --> 22:35.080
It's a hard limit.

22:35.900 --> 22:39.880
The governing physics here involve the square-cube law and diffusion rates in fluid.

22:40.320 --> 22:43.080
So how far can a cell be from a blood vessel before it suffocates?

22:43.420 --> 22:45.380
For a resting muscle, the distance is manageable.

22:45.660 --> 22:50.260
But during heavy exertion, when your cells are burning oxygen rapidly, the math becomes brutal.

22:51.020 --> 22:55.880
Any cell further than about 38 micrometers from a capillary is going to suffocate and die.

22:55.880 --> 22:57.880
38 micrometers.

22:59.140 --> 23:02.440
A human hair is roughly 70 to 100 micrometers thick.

23:02.960 --> 23:10.780
So every single working cell in your bicep or your heart has to be physically located within half a hair's width of a blood vessel.

23:10.780 --> 23:18.500
Which means if you want to build a distributed artificial organ that supports tissue locally, it cannot just sit in a pocket of fat.

23:18.740 --> 23:27.680
It has to physically infiltrate the tissue at a microscopic capillary level, or it has to rely entirely on the body's existing fragile capillary network.

23:27.920 --> 23:28.020
Wow.

23:28.380 --> 23:31.080
You cannot cheat the physics of fluid diffusion.

23:31.080 --> 23:42.840
So if we mathematically cannot shrink a water purifier and put it inside the body, because it will literally cook our organs, our only other option is to move that machinery outside the body.

23:43.140 --> 23:43.460
Yes.

23:43.660 --> 23:48.020
